📗
  🗜 Reads JSON text into a 💠, exactly as RFC 8259 defines JSON and no more
  generously: no comments, no trailing commas, no numbers in other notations.

  It also refuses three things the RFC allows, because each has been used
  against a server: an object with the same key twice, a string with a zero
  character in it, and nesting deeper than it was told to follow. And it
  stops at 20,000 values unless 🧢 says otherwise: a value takes a hundred
  times the memory of the two bytes it can be written in.

    🆕🗜 📇 body❗️ 64❗️ ➡️ reader
    ↪️ 📖 reader❗️ ➡️ value 🍇 … 🍉
    🚩 reader❗️      💭 what was wrong, when there was no value
    📍 reader❗️      💭 and at which byte

  A reader is used once. It goes one level of calls deeper for each level of
  nesting, which is why the depth it is given also bounds its stack.
